ussion interesting, maybe propose Candidate C because of quick decision making and crew atmosphere, but note concerns. Or propose Candidate A for anticipation and leadership. Let's consider criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, calmness, cooperation, responsibility.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality), puts concern for others above everything (crew cooperation), creates positive atmosphere (crew cooperation), but difficulty communicating ideas (communication), egocentric (cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). No explicit stress resilience fact, but quick decisions may imply calmness. No explicit reliability fact, but \"can make correct decisions quickly\" suggests competence.\n\nCandidate A: can anticipate dangerous situations (decision quality, safety), see complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership qualities (crew cooperation, responsibility). Concerns: unorganized (reliability), unfriendly (communication). So strong technical but reliability concerns.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: relatively weak leadership skills, considered moody (crew cooperation). Good overall but leadership weaker.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), concerns: uncooperative? Actually B can be uncooperative and pretentious per notes. Also grumpy. So crew cooperation concerns.\n\nGiven the panel wants best overall fit, maybe Candidate D is balanced: good problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but leadership weak. Candidate B has reliability and stress but crew cooperation issues. Candidate C has crew cooperation but communication issues. Candidate A has technical but reliability issues.\n\nWe could argue for Candidate D as a strong overall, noting strengths across many criteria and that leadership can be developed. Or we could support Candidate B.\n\nSince Agent 3 already recommended B, we could either agree or propose alternative. To stimulate discussion, maybe propose Candidate D, highlighting strengths and noting concerns.\n\nLet's propose Candidate D as provisional recommendation, referencing facts: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), can concentrate very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical competence), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: relatively weak leadership skills, considered moody (crew cooperation). Argue that strengths outweigh concerns and leadership can be trained.\n\nThus we will state recommendation for Candidate D.\n\nWe need to include METADATA_JSON with vote \"Candidate D\".\n\nProceed."}
